--- a/docs/arcane_moments.md
+++ b/docs/arcane_moments.md
@@ -411,3 +411,7 @@ On 16 Oct the site lost all its matches: `/api/matches` returned `Firebase error
 - The list now reads the vote counters stored on each match document (every vote increments them in the same batch), so one list request replaces about 385. A single match (`?matchId=`) still counts its votes exactly.
 - If Firestore refuses a request, the edge function retries 429/503 twice, then serves its last good copy (up to 10 minutes old, header `X-Cache: STALE`) instead of an error.
 - Still heavy, worth fixing later: `/api/total-votes` (founding member threshold) reads every vote document each refresh. It should read the match counters or a single counter document. `/api/live-matches` queries votes per live match, which is fine while few matches are live.
+
+## Edge functions now send the project key (second 429 fix)
+
+After the counters fix `/api/matches` still answered 429 even though the day's reads (32k) were under the Spark cap of 50k. The edge functions called Firestore's REST API anonymously, and Google throttles anonymous calls per source address; Netlify's edge servers share addresses with many other sites. Every Firestore call in `firebase-cache.js`, `live-matches.js` and `profile.js` now goes through `fsFetch()`, which adds the project's public web key (the same one in `js/firebase-config.js`) so the call counts against this project. If the key is ever refused with 403 the call is repeated without it. Errors now include the first part of Firestore's reply, so a 500 says why (`RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED`, rules, key...). If 429 persists, read that text first.
diff --git a/netlify/edge-functions/firebase-cache.js b/netlify/edge-functions/firebase-cache.js
index 9599f80..d2549d8 100644
--- a/netlify/edge-functions/firebase-cache.js
+++ b/netlify/edge-functions/firebase-cache.js
@@ -6,6 +6,27 @@
 const CACHE_DURATION = 30; // 30 seconds
 const STALE_OK_MS = 10 * 60 * 1000; // serve a copy up to 10 minutes old if Firestore refuses
 const FIREBASE_PROJECT = "arcane-moments";
+
+// ---- Firestore REST access --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+// Requests with no API key are anonymous: Google throttles them per source address, and Netlify's edge servers share
+// addresses with many other sites, which is how /api/matches began answering 429 Too Many Requests while the site's own
+// reads (and the daily quota) were fine. Sending the project's public web key makes the call count against this project.
+// If the key were ever blocked (403), the call is repeated without it, so this can only help, never break reads.
+const FIRESTORE_WEB_KEY = "AIzaSyCApnc605mPNOEsAcVnKeXbBTokk7iZP5E";
+const SITE_ORIGIN = "https://arcanemoments.netlify.app/";
+async function fsFetch(url, init = {}) {
+  const keyed = url + (url.includes("?") ? "&" : "?") + "key=" + FIRESTORE_WEB_KEY;
+  const headers = { ...(init.headers || {}), Referer: SITE_ORIGIN };
+  const response = await fetch(keyed, { ...init, headers });
+  if (response.status === 403) return fetch(url, init);
+  return response;
+}
+// Short, safe description of a failed Firestore response, so an error says WHY (quota, rate limit, rules...) and not just the code.
+async function describeFailure(response) {
+  let detail = "";
+  try { detail = (await response.clone().text()).replace(/\s+/g, " ").slice(0, 240); } catch { /* ignore */ }
+  return `${response.status} ${response.statusText}${detail ? " | " + detail : ""}`;
+}
 const TOURNAMENT = "jinx-cup-1";
 
